      "math_academy_id": "q-69950",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a88f6712-2115-55ce-abe4-b3c27e2e105f",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying a Laplace Expansion Across a Row",
      "problem": "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|=$",
      "worked_solution": "The $3$rd row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+2\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
