    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130030",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a88f6712-2115-55ce-abe4-b3c27e2e105f",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying a Laplace Expansion Across a Row",
      "problem": "$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$",
      "worked_solution": "The $4$th row of the matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{42}+1\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
